course: dictionary
lesson: 15382
title: Being exact with specifically
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word specifically means exactly, clearly and in detail. When a writer or speaker wants to be precise, they use it to show that they mean one particular thing. «I specifically asked for no onions.» The speaker is saying: I asked for this exact thing, and nothing else.
explain: Look for clues around the word. «This book was written specifically for children.» The words for children tell you the book was made for one group only. To check your meaning, swap in «just for» or «exactly for». If the sentence still makes sense, you have understood it.
explain: Teachers use specifically to make instructions exact. «Specifically, find three adjectives.» It tells you exactly what to do, so you do not have to guess how many to find.
fact: Specifically means exactly, clearly and in detail.
fact: Specifically can mean for one particular purpose or person only, as in «This game was designed specifically for beginners.»
fact: We use specifically to add a more exact detail, as in «I like animals, specifically cats.»
fact: Useful phrases are more specifically, specifically designed for and specifically asked.
fact: To check the meaning, swap in «exactly» or «in particular». If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «The waiter brought my soup, but I specifically asked for no onions.» What does specifically tell us? | I asked very quietly | I asked for onions in my soup | I was not asked anything at all | *I asked for something exact and clear
exercise: «This puzzle was made specifically for Year 5 children.» Who is the puzzle for? | Children in another country | Grown-ups who like puzzles | *Only Year 5 children | Everyone in the whole school
exercise: Mr Patel says, «Specifically, write three verbs in your notebook.» What should you do? | Write as many words as you like | Draw a picture of a verb | Write a long story | *Write exactly three verbs
test: «I like fruit, specifically apples.» What does specifically add to the sentence? | A sad feeling about apples | A new word for fruit | A question about fruit | *A more exact detail
test: Which word is closest in meaning to specifically in «Tell me specifically what happened»? | Slowly | Happily | *Exactly | Loudly
exam: «The lesson was planned specifically for our class.» What does specifically mean here? | Very quickly | For every school in the country | By accident | *For one particular class only
